3. **Keep the compact atlas.** Full `stop_times` is huge. We already collapse it to patterns + minutes + service days. That is what makes the app fast and hostable. Every new city should emit `public/data/{id}/atlas.json` + `timetable.json` the same way. Lazy-load the city the user is in. Do not merge the planet into one file.

4. **Reuse the same four functions.** `searchAtlas`, `activeServiceIndexes`, `departuresAtStop`, `planTrip` take an atlas, not a city name. Walk-to-métro, àVélo/BIXI, and remote “horaire ailleurs” all sit on those functions. A new agency is new data, not new planner code. When a city has several agencies (STM + exo + REM), ingest each zip with a prefix on IDs and union the stops. That is how we would add STLévis next to RTC.

5. **Bikes are GBFS, same idea.** àVélo and BIXI are discovery URL → `station_information` + `station_status` → merge. The [GBFS systems catalog](https://github.com/MobilityData/gbfs/blob/master/systems.csv) lists the rest of the world. One more `bikes.gbfs` field per city.

6. **When the homemade planner is no longer enough.** For a handful of cities, this compact RAPTOR-lite is the simple path. For “any city, any mode, OSM walking graph,” plug the same ingested GTFS into [MOTIS](https://github.com/motis-project/motis) or [OpenTripPlanner](https://www.opentripplanner.org/) later. Keep our atlas for the map, search, and next passages. Swap only `planTrip` behind the same API. Do not start there.

7. **Licenses stay per agency.** Apache 2.0 is our code. Each GTFS/GBFS row has its own terms. The ingest should store attribution next to the atlas, as we already do for RTC and STM.

