WebGPU on iPhone is now a real drawing API. It is not a replacement for a native iOS app. Safari 26 on iOS 26 / iPadOS 26 ships WebGPU on by default and maps it to Metal, so the map filaments can be fast in the browser. Almost everything else you asked for still needs Swift.

**What WebGPU actually is.** A sandbox GPU API in the page: buffers, shaders (WGSL), compute. Good for drawing thousands of bus lines, dimming the rest, highlighting 801. On Apple silicon it sits on Metal. It is not MapKit, not ActivityKit, not WatchConnectivity, not Foundation Models.

**Where it loses to a native app, for this atlas.**

| Need | WebGPU / Safari | Native (Metal + UIKit/SwiftUI) |
|---|---|---|
| Draw the network | Yes, if iOS 26+ | Yes, older iPhones too, full Metal |
| iOS 18 / older phones | No WebGPU. Fall back to Canvas/WebGL | Fine |
| Apple Maps look (gestures, labels, buildings) | You fake it. No MapKit flyover, no Look Around | MapKit is the real thing |
| Background / lock screen | Tab sleeps. No Live Activity | Live Activities, Dynamic Island, ticks every minute |
| Apple Watch | A webpage cannot drive the Watch | iPhone computes, Watch shows a pulse via WatchConnectivity / mirrored Live Activity |
| On-device Foundation Model | Not exposed to JS | `LanguageModelSession` on device, offline |
| TranslateKit / system languages | You ship string tables | System locale + TranslateKit SDK |
| GPS while locked, significant location | Fragile, permission theater | Standard `CLLocation`, background modes |
| Offline GTFS atlas | Cache/IndexedDB, evicted at will | On-disk, your size, your lifetime |
| Push / “your 801 is in 2 min” | Web Push is limited on iOS | APNs, reliable |
| Haptics, always-on, Digital Crown | No | Yes |
| Battery / thermal | Browser can lose the GPU device, throttle, kill the tab | You control Metal, can pause off-screen |
| Store, Home Screen, share sheet | PWA is second-class on iOS | First-class app |

**GPU-specific limits, even when WebGPU works.** iPhone budgets are smaller than Mac: less buffer memory, smaller workgroups, easier `device.lost`. Safari validates more than a raw Metal app. HDR, some storage textures, and timestamp queries are uneven. Chrome on iOS is still WebKit, so you do not get Chrome’s WebGPU there. A WebView inside your own app can use WebGPU on iOS 26, but it still cannot call Foundation Models or start a Live Activity from JavaScript.

**What is not a WebGPU problem.** Shortest path, next passages, àVélo/BIXI, “horaire ailleurs.” Those are CPU + JSON. An LLM will not beat GTFS math. Foundation Models are for parsing “bus near Youville,” not for Dijkstra.

**The split that matches this project.** Keep the atlas on the web: ingest, search, schedules, pale map, `/Transit`. That is the product you can open on any phone. Put a thin native shell around it when you care about Apple-only output: Live Activity on the iPhone, a Watch face that is a live pulse (not a cloned app), on-device query parse. WebGPU is the optional pretty layer on top of the same data. Native is how that data stays on your wrist when Safari is not in the foreground.

