Both exist. WebGPU wins on reach and shipping speed. Native wins on Apple-only surfaces. Neither replaces the GTFS atlas.

**Advantages of WebGPU (in Safari / a WebView)**

- One codebase. Québec and Montréal (and later any GTFS city) stay the same page on iPhone, iPad, Mac, Android, desktop.
- Ships without the App Store. `/Transit` is a URL. No review, no binary, no version lag.
- Fast enough for this map. Safari 26 on iOS 26 maps WebGPU to Metal. Drawing route filaments, dimming the rest, highlighting 801 is a good WebGPU job.
- Same GPU model as the rest of the web. Chrome, Edge, Safari 26. Fallback is Canvas 2D, which we already have.
- No extra native team for the core product: ingest, search, horaires ailleurs, walk/BIXI/àVélo, planner.
- Easier to host and update. New STM zip is `npm run ingest`, not a new TestFlight.

**Limitations of WebGPU versus a native iOS app**

- OS gate. Real WebGPU on iPhone is Safari 26 / iOS 26+. Older phones get Canvas/WebGL only.
- It only draws. It does not give you MapKit, Live Activities, the Watch, Foundation Models, TranslateKit, APNs, or background location.
- A tab is not an app. Safari can suspend it, lose the GPU device (`device.lost`), throttle thermals, evict the 17 MB Montréal timetable from cache.
- No Watch. A webpage cannot push a live pulse to the paired watch. Native WatchConnectivity / a mirrored Live Activity can.
- No lock-screen “next 801 in 3 min” while the phone is in a pocket. That is ActivityKit.
- On-device Apple Intelligence is Swift-only. JS cannot call `LanguageModelSession`.
- Gestures, haptics, Digital Crown, always-on display: native.
- Memory and workgroup limits are tighter on iPhone than on Mac, and tighter than raw Metal in your own process.
- Chrome on iOS is still WebKit. You do not get Google’s WebGPU stack on iPhone.

**Advantages of native iOS**

- Live Activities, Dynamic Island, complications, Watch face fed by the iPhone.
- Foundation Models on device, offline, for “horaire Youville” parse.
- MapKit if you want actual Apple Maps cartography instead of a custom or OSM layer.
- Reliable background ticks and push.
- Full Metal, no browser validator in the way.
- Feels like a system app: Home Screen, share sheet, Siri, App Intents.

**Limitations of native**

- Two (or three) codebases if you still want the web atlas. Swift does not run the Next.js planner for free.
- App Store, provisioning, watchOS companion target, OS version matrix.
- You still need the same GTFS ingest. Native does not invent STM schedules.
- Slower to extend to “any city”: the catalog loop we have is a folder of JSON, not a new binary per agency.

**For Rive, the honest split**

Use WebGPU where it has an advantage: drawing the network in the browser, one URL, every city is more data. Use native where WebGPU has a hard no: Watch pulse, Live Activity, on-device model. Keep path math and horaires on the official GTFS. That work is not a GPU problem and not an LLM problem.

