## Assistant

WebGPU is not a substitute for a native iPhone app. It is a GPU API that now ships in Safari 26 on iOS 26, iPadOS 26, macOS 26, and visionOS 26, and maps onto Metal. For this atlas, almost all of the product (stop search, day timetable, RAPTOR-lite plan, àVélo/BIXI) is CPU work. The map already draws in Canvas 2D; `tryWebGPU()` only labels the corner “WebGPU prêt” or “Canvas 2D”. So the useful comparison is **Safari + optional WebGPU** versus **Swift/Metal + Watch + Apple Intelligence**.

There are real limitations, and real advantages. Both matter.

## Limitations (WebGPU / web vs native)

**Apple surfaces WebGPU cannot touch**
- **Apple Watch.** No Safari, no WebGPU, no real Watch app. `watch.html` is a same-origin page, not a paired pulse. WatchConnectivity, complications, and always-on glance only exist in a native Watch target.
- **Foundation Models.** On-device Apple Intelligence is Swift-only. The web cannot call it for messy “de / vers” queries.
- **Live Activities, Dynamic Island, widgets, App Intents / Siri.** Next-bus on the Lock Screen is native.
- **Background location and refresh.** A PWA cannot do the same always-on geofence or silent GTFS refresh a native app can.

**The GPU API itself is a subset of Metal**
- No Metal Performance Shaders, MetalFX, or the full argument-buffer / mesh-shader surface.
- iPhone/iPad GPU memory and workgroup limits are tighter than on a Mac.
- The browser can lose the GPU device when the tab backgrounds or the phone gets hot.
- Full default support is **Safari 26 / iOS 26**. Older iPhones stay on Canvas 2D. Chrome on iOS is still WebKit, so it does not escape that gate.

**Distribution and OS privileges**
- No Home Screen widget, no reliable background fetch, weaker offline than a native container.
- GBFS/CORS and file access stay inside the browser sandbox.
- You cannot ship a real Watch companion from GitHub Pages.

**For this product, WebGPU is not the bottleneck.** RTC + STM is thousands of stops and polyline shapes. Search and planning dominate. A Metal rewrite would not make “horaire ailleurs” faster in a way a rider would feel.

## Advantages (WebGPU / web vs native)

**The ones that already match the brief**
- **One URL, no fee, no store.** `https://thebonhomme.com/transit` on Safari iPhone, iPad, and Mac. Push Pages and everyone has the new grid. That is the “no Transit paywall” product.
- **Share a stop, not an install.** Day-one feature: look up a schedule at a stop you are not at.
- **Same planner everywhere.** One `atlas.json` + `timetable.json` + JS RAPTOR. Native would be SwiftUI + Watch + a web copy, or a WKWebView shell around the same JS.
- **Fallback is already designed.** iOS 18 users still get Canvas 2D. Native-or-nothing would lock you to Xcode and a signed device.

**What WebGPU specifically buys (when you actually use it)**
- Apple now treats it as the successor to WebGL; the API is Metal-shaped, not OpenGL-shaped, so less translation tax.
- **Compute shaders** — something WebGL never had. Later you can GPU-nearest-stop, densify/decimate shapes, or heat-map a city without rewriting in Swift.
- Graphics workloads that fit the API typically land in the high-80s to mid-90s percent of native GPU, which is enough for a pale Apple-Maps-style atlas.
- One WGSL path runs on Mac Safari and iPhone 26+. Metal is Apple-only.

**Operational**
- Apache 2.0 on your code, official GTFS, no Apple review for the live atlas.
- Any-city ingest stays a static publish. A native app still needs a server or an App Store update for new cities.

## What this means for Rive

| Need | Winner |
|---|---|
| Horaire ailleurs, De/Vers, àVélo/BIXI, live URL | Web |
| Dense map / GPU search later | WebGPU on iOS 26+, Canvas 2D otherwise |
| Watch pulse from the paired iPhone | Native |
| Foundation Models for hard search | Native |
| Lock Screen next-bus | Native |
| $0, instant update, any city | Web |

Keep the site as the product. Treat WebGPU as an accelerator for drawing or compute, not as the app. If you want Watch + Apple Intelligence + Live Activities, that is a thin native shell around the same atlas, not a Metal rewrite of the timetable.

If you want a next step, the high-leverage one is that native shell (sideload, no $99), not more WebGPU.

